Every repeated value cancels itself with XOR because `x ^ x` is zero. XORing zero with the value that appears once leaves that value. The sorted order is not needed for this method.

Dry run for `[4, 4, 7, 8, 8]`:

```text
4 ^ 4 ^ 7 ^ 8 ^ 8 = 0 ^ 7 ^ 0 = 7
```

This uses constant extra space, but it still visits the whole array, so it is not the logarithmic option.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int singleNonDuplicate(vector<int> &nums) {
        int answer = 0;

        for (int number : nums) {
            answer ^= number;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

A frequency map gives a direct solution. Count each number, then scan the original array and return the one whose count is one. Scanning `nums` again keeps the result simple and also preserves the array order.

The input is guaranteed to contain one answer, so the final `-1` is only a fallback. This version needs extra memory, unlike the binary search solution.

```java
class Solution {
    public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        java.util.HashMap<Integer, Integer> counts = new java.util.HashMap<>();

        for (int number : nums) {
            counts.put(number, counts.getOrDefault(number, 0) + 1);
        }

        for (int number : nums) {
            if (counts.get(number) == 1) {
                return number;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(n)`

The pairs are next to each other because the array is sorted. Starting at index 0, compare every element with the next one. The first mismatch gives the answer. If all pairs match, the last value is the single one.

For example, with `[1, 1, 2, 3, 3]`, the pair at index 2 is broken, so `3` is returned. This is easy to follow, but it does not use the sorted array for a logarithmic search.

```python
class Solution:
    def singleNonDuplicate(self, nums):
        for index in range(0, len(nums) - 1, 2):
            if nums[index] != nums[index + 1]:
                return nums[index]
        return nums[-1]
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Before the single value, pairs occupy `(even index, odd index)` positions. Once the single value is passed, this pattern shifts. I make `mid` even and compare it with `mid + 1`.

If those two values match, the single value must be farther right. If they do not match, it is at `mid` or somewhere to the left.

For `[1, 1, 2, 3, 3]`, checking the pair at indices 2 and 3 fails, so the search keeps index 2 and the left side.

```java
class Solution {
    public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (mid % 2 == 1) {
                mid--;
            }

            if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) {
                left = mid + 2;
            } else {
                right = mid;
            }
        }

        return nums[left];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The middle value can be checked against either neighbor. A normal pair is at an even index followed by its copy, or at an odd index preceded by its copy. That tells which half still contains the unpaired value.

The boundary cases are covered by checking the first and last positions before using both neighbors. For a one-item array, the only item is returned immediately. The input list is not changed.

```python
class Solution:
    def singleNonDuplicate(self, nums):
        length = len(nums)

        if length == 1:
            return nums[0]
        if nums[0] != nums[1]:
            return nums[0]
        if nums[-1] != nums[-2]:
            return nums[-1]

        left = 1
        right = length - 2

        while left <= right:
            mid = left + (right - left) // 2

            if nums[mid] != nums[mid - 1] and nums[mid] != nums[mid + 1]:
                return nums[mid]

            pair_is_aligned = (
                (mid % 2 == 0 and nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) or
                (mid % 2 == 1 and nums[mid] == nums[mid - 1])
            )

            if pair_is_aligned:
                left = mid + 1
            else:
                right = mid - 1

        return -1
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

## Intuition
Just perform simple comparisons between elements in the array. During iteration, for each element, check if it is equal to next element. If it is equal increment i by 2 else return nums[i].


## Solution
